::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Why are definitions important in Geometry?
{
~A. They make diagrams unnecessary.#Definitions support diagram reasoning, but diagrams can still provide important information.
~B. They allow students to guess from appearance.#Definitions reduce guessing by identifying the features that matter.
~C. They replace the need for explanations.#Definitions support explanations, but students still need to connect definitions to evidence.
=D. They give exact meanings for mathematical words.#Correct. Definitions help students use Geometry language consistently and precisely.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which object is an exact location usually named with a capital letter?
{
=A. Point#Correct. A point is an exact location and is usually named with a capital letter.
~B. Line#A line is a straight path that extends forever in both directions.
~C. Segment#A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints.
~D. Angle#An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A straight path extends forever in both directions. Which object is described?
{
~A. Ray#A ray extends in one direction from one endpoint, not in both directions.
~B. Segment#A segment has two endpoints and does not extend forever.
=C. Line#Correct. A line extends forever in both directions.
~D. Point#A point is a location, not an extended straight path.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which object has one endpoint and extends forever in one direction?
{
=A. Ray#Correct. A ray has one endpoint and extends forever in one direction.
~B. Line#A line extends forever in both directions and has no endpoints.
~C. Segment#A segment has two endpoints.
~D. Angle#An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which object has two endpoints?
{
~A. Point#A point is a single location and does not have two endpoints.
=B. Segment#Correct. A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints.
~C. Line#A line extends forever in both directions and has no endpoints.
~D. Ray#A ray has one endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which object is formed by two rays with a common endpoint?
{
~A. Line#A line is one straight path extending in both directions.
~B. Segment#A segment has two endpoints and is not formed by two rays.
~C. Point#A point is a location, not a figure formed by rays.
=D. Angle#Correct. An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A figure begins at point M and continues through point N in one direction. Which description is most precise?
{
~A. Line MN#A line extends in both directions and has no endpoint.
~B. Segment MN#A segment has two endpoints, but the figure continues in one direction.
=C. Ray MN#Correct. Ray MN starts at M and continues through N in one direction.
~D. Angle MN#An angle is formed by two rays and usually requires three letters to name clearly.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A figure has endpoints R and S. Which description is most precise?
{
~A. Line RS#A line has no endpoints and extends in both directions.
=B. Segment RS#Correct. A segment has two endpoints, so endpoints R and S identify segment RS.
~C. Ray RS#A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction.
~D. Angle RS#An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint and is not named with only two letters here.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Rays PA and PB share endpoint P. Which statement is most precise?
{
=A. Rays PA and PB form angle APB with vertex P.#Correct. This statement names the rays, angle, and vertex precisely.
~B. There is a corner near P.#This is informal and does not identify the rays or angle.
~C. PA and PB are the same segment.#Rays are not segments, and two different rays are being described.
~D. P is just part of the drawing.#This does not explain the geometric relationship or the shared endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
In angle DEF, which point is the vertex?
{
~A. D#D is on one ray of the angle, but it is not the middle letter.
~B. F#F is on one ray of the angle, but it is not the vertex.
=C. E#Correct. The middle letter names the vertex, so E is the vertex of angle DEF.
~D. D and F#D and F help name the rays, but the shared endpoint is E.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which statement is vague rather than precise?
{
~A. Segment AB has endpoints A and B.#This uses precise language and identifies the endpoints.
~B. Ray CD begins at C and passes through D.#This describes the ray precisely.
~C. Line EF extends in both directions.#This uses precise language about a line.
=D. The figure has a corner over there.#Correct. This is vague because it does not identify the object, point, rays, or angle.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A student says, "This is a line because it looks straight." What is missing from the reasoning?
{
=A. Evidence that the figure extends in both directions#Correct. A line is straight and extends forever in both directions, so extension evidence matters.
~B. A color description#Color is not needed to identify a line.
~C. A guess about length#Guessing length does not define a line.
~D. A statement that it has two endpoints#Two endpoints would describe a segment, not a line.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A student calls a figure a segment because it looks short. What misconception is shown?
{
~A. The student used endpoints correctly.#No endpoints are mentioned. The student relied on appearance.
~B. The student identified a vertex.#A vertex is not part of this reasoning.
~C. The student proved the figure was a line.#The student did not prove anything about a line.
=D. The student used appearance instead of a definition.#Correct. A segment is identified by two endpoints, not by how short the drawing looks.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which sentence correctly revises "The shape has a corner at B"?
{
~A. The thing turns at B.#This remains vague and does not name a geometric object.
=B. Rays BA and BC form angle ABC with vertex B.#Correct. This precise sentence names the rays, the angle, and the vertex.
~C. B is where it bends.#This is informal and does not identify rays or an angle.
~D. B looks like the middle.#This relies on appearance and does not use precise Geometry language.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which statement best explains the difference between observation and evidence?
{
~A. An observation is always proof.#An observation may help, but proof or conclusion requires supporting evidence.
~B. Evidence is only a guess from the picture.#Evidence is not a guess; it supports a conclusion.
=C. An observation is something noticed; evidence is information used to support a conclusion.#Correct. Evidence uses observations, labels, definitions, markings, or given statements to support a conclusion.
~D. Observation and evidence mean the same thing in Geometry.#They are related, but they are not the same.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which option is evidence that a figure is a ray?
{
=A. It has one endpoint and an arrow showing extension in one direction.#Correct. These features match the definition of a ray.
~B. It looks shorter than the other figures.#Length by appearance does not define a ray.
~C. It is drawn near the top of the page.#Location on the page does not define a geometric object.
~D. It has two endpoints and no arrows.#That describes a segment, not a ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which option is evidence that a figure is a segment?
{
~A. It has arrows on both ends.#Arrows on both ends suggest a line, not a segment.
=B. It has two endpoints.#Correct. A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints.
~C. It has one endpoint and one arrow.#That describes a ray.
~D. It is drawn horizontally.#Orientation does not define a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which option is evidence that a figure is a line?
{
~A. It has one endpoint.#A figure with one endpoint is not a line.
~B. It has two endpoints.#A figure with two endpoints is a segment.
=C. It is straight and has arrows showing it extends in both directions.#Correct. A line is straight and extends forever in both directions.
~D. It looks long.#Length by appearance does not define a line.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which statement uses a definition correctly?
{
~A. A line has two endpoints.#A line has no endpoints and extends in both directions.
~B. A segment extends forever in one direction.#A segment has two endpoints and does not extend forever.
~C. A point is formed by two rays.#An angle is formed by two rays; a point is an exact location.
=D. A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction.#Correct. This is the definition of a ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which statement uses appearance instead of evidence?
{
~A. Segment AB has endpoints A and B.#This uses endpoint evidence.
=B. The figure is a segment because it looks short.#Correct. This relies on appearance instead of the defining feature of two endpoints.
~C. Ray CD has endpoint C and extends through D.#This uses defining features of a ray.
~D. Line EF has arrows on both ends.#This uses diagram evidence for extension in both directions.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q21
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which response best supports the conclusion "the figure is segment JK"?
{
=A. The figure has endpoints J and K.#Correct. A segment has two endpoints, so this evidence supports the conclusion.
~B. It is near the center of the diagram.#Position in the diagram does not support segment identification.
~C. It looks like a small line.#Appearance is not enough. The definition matters.
~D. It passes through point J.#Passing through one point is not enough to identify a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q22
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which response best supports the conclusion "the figure is ray TU"?
{
~A. It has endpoints T and U.#Two endpoints describe a segment, not a ray.
~B. It extends in both directions through T and U.#That describes a line, not a ray.
~C. It is the shortest figure shown.#Length by appearance does not identify a ray.
=D. It starts at T and continues through U in one direction.#Correct. A ray has one endpoint and extends through another point in one direction.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q23
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
Which response best supports the conclusion "the figure is line VW"?
{
~A. It has endpoints V and W.#Endpoints identify a segment, not a line.
~B. It starts at V and ends at W.#A line does not start or end at those points.
=C. It is straight and extends in both directions through V and W.#Correct. A line is straight and extends forever in both directions.
~D. It looks straight and short.#Looking straight is not enough, and shortness does not define a line.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q24
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
A student writes, "A ray has two endpoints." What is the correct revision?
{
=A. A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction.#Correct. This is the definition of a ray.
~B. A ray has no endpoints and extends in both directions.#That describes a line, not a ray.
~C. A ray is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.#That describes an angle.
~D. A ray is an exact location.#That describes a point.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U01_L02_Q25
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
Question:
What is the main purpose of this readiness lesson?
{
~A. To assign a formal Geometry benchmark#This lesson is readiness support only and does not introduce a new primary MLA standard.
=B. To use precise definitions and evidence when describing geometric objects#Correct. The lesson focuses on precise language, definitions, observation, evidence, and avoiding appearance-based conclusions.
~C. To prove advanced theorems about figures#Formal proof develops later. This lesson builds language and definition habits first.
~D. To replace diagrams with memorized formulas#This lesson uses diagrams and definitions, not formula memorization.
}

